{"cluster_id": "C-3100", "topic": "part lifecycle claim", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-418"], "canonical_claim": "A thread-local \"active slot\" design for the part-folder cache was rejected as unnecessary v1 complexity with no measurable benefit over a bounded shared map lookup.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-08-cas-part-folder-cache-design.md#rejected-alternatives"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3101", "topic": "part lifecycle claim", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-082"], "canonical_claim": "The part-write crash-point table enumerates nine distinct crash windows (C1-C9) and their cleanup owner: C1 staging leaves local temp files (cleaned unconditionally) or S3 staging objects (cleaned only by the mount's own next-mount staging sweep, never on abort); C2 post-stageManifest-pre-precommitAdd leaves an unreferenced manifest cleaned by best-effort delete or the orphan sweep backstop; C3 an Unresolved precommitAdd leaves a possibly-live binding whose body is never writer-deleted; C4 between precommitAdd and promote has no resume path (INV-10) and is removed by abandon or a fenced successor's stale-precommit sweep; C5 mid blob fan-out leaves already-uploaded blobs as GC-reclaimable debris with nothing merged; C6 an Unresolved promote append leaves `CommitState::Uncertain`, mapped by the relink layer to \"retry the whole fetch\"; C7 a later part throwing after earlier parts published triggers precise rollback of only the refs this call created; C8 a destroyed uncommitted transaction has its destructor abandon every open build (INV-19); C9 a namespace dropped mid-build sets one atomic flag so every further op fails closed at `requireAlive`. Every row leaks at worst; no row loses data or dangles.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/11-walkthrough.md#crash-points"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3102", "topic": "part lifecycle claim", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M-002"], "canonical_claim": "Free carry-forward on mutation (re-referencing unchanged column blobs without recompute) applies only to Wide parts, whose default remote-disk threshold is 1 GiB; Compact parts are a single blob and are always fully rewritten on mutation.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/01-architecture.md#what-it-does-not-buy"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3103", "topic": "part lifecycle claim", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B015-058"], "canonical_claim": "S36 is commissioned to test `ALTER TABLE ... MOVE PART|PARTITION TO DISK/VOLUME` in both local-to-CA and CA-to-local directions, including no dangling refs and round-trip deduplication.", "issue_ids": ["B21", "S36"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-13-scenarios-stabilization-status.md#new-scenarios"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3104", "topic": "part lifecycle claim", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B043-095"], "canonical_claim": "The `B92` adopt path must populate the correct `tree_size` instead of leaving it at zero.", "issue_ids": ["B92"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/random_notes.md#lines-173-181"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3105", "topic": "ReadBufferFromFileView position rebasing", "target": "architecture/read-path", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-056", "B002-122", "B003-107", "B032-075", "B035-094", "B051-023", "B051-024"], "canonical_claim": "`ReadBufferFromFileView` must rebase position accounting from the implementation's real post-operation state and restore buffer swaps on exceptions; otherwise range reads can drift or retain swapped buffers. Additionally, `ReadBufferFromFileView` could leave `file_offset_of_buffer_end` inconsistent when its inner buffer reset its working buffer during `setReadUntilPosition`, making `getPosition` incorrect and allowing seek or mark logic to reread an old decompressed block. Additionally, After `impl->setReadUntilPosition`, `ReadBufferFromFileView` must recalculate its position using `impl->getPosition()` and `impl->available()` when the inner buffer may reset its working buffer. Additionally, The B115 fix rebases `file_offset_of_buffer_end` from `impl->getPosition() + impl->available()` after every inner-buffer operation, including `next`, `seek`, `setReadUntilPosition`, and `setReadUntilEnd`. Additionally, The B115 `ReadBufferFromFileView` fix recomputes its offset from the inner buffer after every right-bound change because `ReadBufferFromS3` resets its working buffer; the same change includes `ReadBufferFromMemory` coverage. Additionally, The B115 `ReadBufferFromFileView` position-corruption bug was latent (not yet triggered) in `PackedFilesReader`, the only other in-tree consumer of `ReadBufferFromFileView`, because its current caller (`loadStatisticsPacked`) uses a sequential, non-seekable `CompressedReadBuffer` that never calls `setReadUntilPosition`; the fix is in place there too and the report flags this as upstream-relevant.; The read-path gap-fill recovered B59 projection readback, its `tryGetInFlight*` overlay, directory-granularity overlay rules, and B115 `ReadBufferFromFileView` position rebasing into `09-read-protocol.md`; B115 passed 36/36 gtests after the fix versus 14/36 before it.", "issue_ids": ["B115", "B59", "B63"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/09-read-protocol.md#L235-L237", "docs/superpowers/cas/09-read-protocol.md#L465", "docs/superpowers/cas/09-read-protocol.md#b115-fix", "docs/superpowers/cas/CONSOLIDATION-COVERAGE.md#read-path-gap-fill", "docs/superpowers/cas/upstream.md#L176-L188", "docs/superpowers/cas/upstream.md#L183-L187", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-03-cas-carve-script.md#L236-L250", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/05-integration.md#4-general-infra-additions-class-b-mini-specs"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3106", "topic": "cache over CA disk", "target": "architecture/read-path", "member_ids": ["B007-050", "B007-087", "B007-089", "B009-025", "B009-026"], "canonical_claim": "Before the cache-over-CA fix, a `<type>cache</type>` disk over CA failed closed at `IDisk::startup` with code 48 `NOT_IMPLEMENTED` because the generic cache metadata wrapper hid the CA write path. Additionally, A cache disk over a content-addressed disk originally failed at startup because MetadataStorageFromCacheObjectStorage did not forward isContentAddressed or the content-addressed transaction surface. Additionally, For a content-addressed disk, `DiskObjectStorage::wrapWithCache` reuses the CA metadata storage and wraps only the object storage with `CachedObjectStorage`. Additionally, Under cache-over-CA, immutable content-hash blobs are read through `CachedObjectStorage`, while refs, manifests, GC, and writes use the CA metadata storage's raw uncached object-storage pointer. Additionally, The cache-over-content-addressed fix reuses the content-addressed metadata storage directly and wraps only the object storage layer.", "issue_ids": ["S3"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-08-cas-file-cache-disk-support.md#architecture", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-08-cas-file-cache-disk-support-design.md#data-flow", "docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-06-scenario-validation-night.md#s3-cache-disk", "docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-08-unattended-cas-cache-and-stabilization.md#L1-L38"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3107", "topic": "inline entry size", "target": "architecture/read-path", "member_ids": ["B018-068", "B018-069", "B018-070"], "canonical_claim": "The 02941 projection failure was caused by `tryGetInFlightFileSize` and `tryGetInFlightStorageObjects` returning `blob_size` for an Inline entry, even though manifest decoding leaves Inline `blob_size` at zero and stores the real size in `inline_bytes`. Additionally, The durable fix for the Inline-size bug was to return `inline_bytes.size()` for Inline entries, add the regression test `CaTransactionLockScope.InFlightReadCarriedForwardProjectionSidecar`, and preserve `blob_size` for Blob entries. Additionally, The later hardening added canonical `ManifestEntry::size()` and routed all size consumers through it, eliminating duplicated Inline-versus-Blob size ternaries while retaining legitimate Blob-object byte sites.", "issue_ids": ["B58", "B63"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-16-unattended-codecs-txn-sourcelayout-part1.md#L61-L80"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3108", "topic": "reader-executor file_view fallback", "target": "architecture/read-path", "member_ids": ["B019-072", "B019-104", "B021-035", "B033-095"], "canonical_claim": "Review finding \u211616 is real: `ReadPipeline::tryBuildReaderExecutor` does not fall back for `file_view`, so a CA read can expose the `CHCA` envelope header and shift offsets when `use_reader_executor=1` and `remote_filesystem_read_method='read'`. Additionally, The CAS review triage confirmed that `ReaderExecutor` could bypass the `file_view` payload window and return wrong results; task T7 fixed the fallback condition in commit `aaf61086527`. Additionally, With `use_reader_executor=1`, CAS reads can expose the envelope header and use incorrect offsets because `ReadPipeline` rejects unsupported cache/decryption stages but not `file_view`. Additionally, `ReadPipeline::tryBuildReaderExecutor` must include `file_view` in its fallback condition until the executor supports CA payload windows.", "issue_ids": ["T7", "\u211616"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-17-codex-triage-fix-wave.md#L144-L159", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-17-codex-review-triage.md#L35-L36", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-17-codex-review-triage.md#L84-L96", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-19-session-full-summary.md#L10-L21", "docs/superpowers/reports/20260717_codex_review.md#high-severity"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3109", "topic": "failed AsyncLoader recovery", "target": "architecture/read-path", "member_ids": ["B020-050", "B021-067", "B021-068", "B021-078", "B021-079", "B022-001", "B022-002", "B022-003", "B022-006", "B022-027"], "canonical_claim": "A five-hour CA soak failed when transient RustFS I/O saturation caused a CAS recovery-seal `NETWORK_ERROR`, after which `AsyncLoader` permanently cached the `ATTACH TABLE` failure without an automatic retry. Additionally, A transient S3 failure during CAS ref-table startup recovery must not permanently strand the table until server restart. Additionally, The stuck-table-load design has two layers: `CasRefLedger::ensureRefTableRecovered` retries transient recovery errors with bounded backoff, while CAS tables are placed in a database created with `lazy_load_tables = 1` so a failed load is retried on the next access. Additionally, With `lazy_load_tables = 1`, a table attaches as a `StorageTableProxy`; if its first access fails because RustFS is unavailable, the error is per-query rather than a cached `ASYNC_LOAD_WAIT_FAILED`, and a later access retries without a server restart. Additionally, Generic `AsyncLoader` marks a failed async table-load job as terminal and later accesses rethrow the cached exception; `DETACH TABLE` can hit a catch-22 because table resolution waits for the failed job before reaching `eraseAsyncLoadState`. Additionally, A dense chaos-fault burst during CAS table-startup recovery caused the recovery seal PUT to exhaust its request envelope, after which `AsyncLoader` cached the table's `load table` job as `FAILED` and every later table touch rethrew the cached exception until a full server restart. Additionally, `AsyncLoader` has no retry, requeue, or reset path for a `FAILED` job; failure is terminal and the stored exception is rethrown by later waits. Additionally, `DETACH TABLE` cannot clear a failed asynchronous table-load state because table resolution waits on the failed load before reaching `DatabaseOrdinary::detachTableUnlocked`, where `eraseAsyncLoadState` is called. Additionally, The CAS recovery fix has two independent layers: bounded retry for short backend outages and retry-on-touch or equivalent recovery after a load failure, because any finite startup retry can be outlasted by a real outage. Additionally, A transient backend failure during `AsyncLoader`-driven table or disk startup can permanently strand the table as `FAILED`; `DETACH` and `ATTACH` do not retry it, and only a server restart recovers it.", "issue_ids": ["finding-6"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-20-cas-table-load-stuck-asyncloader.md#L5-L5", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-20-cas-table-load-stuck-asyncloader.md#L5-L8", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-20-cas-table-load-stuck-asyncloader.md#L672-L680", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-20-cas-table-load-stuck-asyncloader.md#L808-L843", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-20-umbrella-review-cas-vs-antalya-26.6.md#major-issues", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-20-umbrella-review-fixes-deferred.md#startup-retry", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-20-upstream-issue-draft-asyncloader-stuck-table.md#root-cause-current-master", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-20-cas-table-load-stuck-asyncloader-design.md#background", "docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-17-unattended-r5-r6-round.md#L490-L511"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3110", "topic": "read path claim", "target": "architecture/read-path", "member_ids": ["B022-028"], "canonical_claim": "`DiskObjectStorage::prepareRead` downcasts to concrete `ContentAddressedMetadataStorage`, bypassing the `IContentAddressedExchange` decorator seam and forcing generic cache wrappers to be bypassed for CA disks.", "issue_ids": ["finding-7"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-20-umbrella-review-fixes.md#task-15-route-prepareread-ca-hooks-through-icontentaddressedexchange", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-20-umbrella-review-cas-vs-antalya-26.6.md#major-issues"]}
